  "headline": "A hot economy priced through the rates market, not a stagflation scare",
  "body": [
    "The configuration reads as reflation being met by a hawkish repricing. Growth signals are strong: copper sits at 95% of its 52-week range and up 20% year-to-date, Brent is up 35% over three months, and the copper/gold ratio has risen 11% on the month. Risk appetite is intact, with bitcoin up 40% over three months and now buying 15% more gold than a month ago. That is a liquidity-and-growth mix, not fear.",
    "The bond market is doing the disciplining. The 10-year yield sits at a 52-week high of 5.18%, up 52 basis points over the month, while the 30-year has risen only 32 basis points; the 30y–10y spread has flattened 20 basis points. A curve that flattens as it rises says the market is pricing tighter policy to contain inflation, not a runaway long-term inflation premium. Gold's 7% drop over the month against rising yields confirms it: real rates are biting, and investors are not buying protection.",
    "The tension is the dollar. Reflation usually softens it, yet the index is up 1.9% on the month near its range high and USD/INR is up 2.4%. That fits a US-rate story, with capital drawn to 5% Treasury yields. Today's 8% oil slide and firmer yen are noise inside a choppy oil regime rather than a change of read."
  ],
